    "etymology": {
     "text": "Univerbation of هَا (attention particle) + ذَا; cognate with Hebrew הַזֶּה (hazé, “this”).",
     "src": "Wiktionary (kaikki.org)"
    },
    "funFacts": [
     "Hebrew הַזֶּה (hazé) and Arabic هٰذَا share the same deictic element: Arabic ذ = Hebrew ז."
    ],
    "pitfalls": [
     "The first ā is written with a small (dagger) alif, not a full one: هٰذَا. Unvocalized it is just هذا.",
     "هٰذَا الْبَصَلُ = this onion (a phrase); هٰذَا بَصَلٌ = this is onion (a sentence). The article makes the difference."
    ],
    "subtleties": [
     "Non-human plurals take the feminine singular: هٰذِهِ جَزَرَاتٌ (these are carrots); هٰؤُلَاءِ is only for people."
    ]
